{
  "texts": [
    {
      "content": "The Sunspire develops a yellow basal bulb from which rigid red and yellow leaves emerge, arranged in a vertical tuft. ",
      "left": "557px",
      "top": "265px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "26px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Its morphology indicates adaptation to open areas with high exposure to Maxkodia’s twin suns.",
      "left": "663px",
      "top": "310px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "26px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Field analyses reveal it accumulates carotenoid pigments in unusual amounts, likely to dissipate excess radiation.",
      "left": "613px",
      "top": "358px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "26px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "It partially folds its leaves during intense blue-light phases, reopening them under the red cycle.",
      "left": "660px",
      "top": "403px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "26px",
